    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d366">

                <pb facs="0916" n="868" xml:id="pg868"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.5 MSP/3–1152</note>
                <head>No. 366<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Secretary Eden to the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d366-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-03-10">March 10, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>You will have seen the Prime Minister’s message to The President<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d366-f2"/> explaining the economic circumstances in which we find
                    ourselves and making the suggestion that the United States should take over some
                    of our United Kingdom contracts for the production of the most valuable N.A.T.O. weapons (Venoms, Canberras and the
                    like).</p>
                <p>I mentioned to you in Lisbon how seriously concerned I was over our economic
                        problems.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d366-f3"/> The measures which we are now
                    forced to undertake in connexion with the Budget show our firm determination to
                    put our affairs in order. I know you understand how essential it is for us if we
                    are to play our full part in Western defence to have a sound economy and I am
                    sending you this message to enlist your sympathetic support for the proposal
                    which the Prime Minister is now making.</p>
                <p>I realize that it will raise a good many difficulties especially for Bob
                        Lovett and Averell. But I am convinced that
                    something on these lines is necessary and to our mutual interest. I am sure we
                    can rely on you and your colleagues to do your utmost to help.</p>


                <xi:include href="d366-a1.xml" xpointer="d366-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d366-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d366-f1-content">
                <p>This message was delivered to Secretary Acheson by Ambassador Franks on Mar. 11 and was attached to a memorandum by Battle to the White House, along with
                        Churchill’s message to
                        Truman (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >supra</hi>), which asked that they be cabled to the President who was then
                    in Florida.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d366-f2" n="2" xml:id="d366-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d366-f3" n="3" xml:id="d366-f3-content">
                <p>For a record of Eden’s discussion with
                        Acheson on British economic
                    problems during the Ninth Session of the North Atlantic Council at Lisbon, Feb.
                    20–25, see <ref target="frus1952-54v05p1#pg_131">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, Part 1, p. 131</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
